WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:01:02.235 --> 00:01:04.456
The classroom was supposed to be at the back,

00:01:04.716 --> 00:01:05.358
for girls,

00:01:05.897 --> 00:01:06.737
She couldn't find it.

00:01:07.137 --> 00:01:12.721
I know some people who thought it was big,

00:01:12.881 --> 00:01:13.661
I remember that.

00:01:14.382 --> 00:01:15.542
and then the gates.

00:01:19.104 --> 00:01:21.605
It's a source of both pride and sadness to see all this.

00:01:21.805 --> 00:01:23.846
because it represents so much,

00:01:23.947 --> 00:01:24.966
So much work.

00:01:29.024 --> 00:01:37.648
Here we are in the ruins of the extraordinary building that was the Troyes sanatorium.

00:01:37.668 --> 00:01:40.329
This building will soon be 100 years old.

00:01:40.349 --> 00:01:47.592
since a large part of it was built between 1928 and 1932.

00:01:47.593 --> 00:01:53.594
And this is the work of Maurice Violette, who was mayor from 1908 to 1960.

00:01:53.634 --> 00:01:55.675
You see,
and he was a great builder.

00:02:40.156 --> 00:02:42.158
All these children who fill the schools of large cities,

00:02:42.198 --> 00:02:42.959
Look at them closely.

00:02:43.118 --> 00:02:43.879
Listen to them too,

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:45.861
Listen to these disturbing things.

00:02:46.301 --> 00:02:47.804
Look at these ashen faces.

00:02:48.284 --> 00:02:49.526
Tuberculosis is everywhere.

00:02:49.966 --> 00:02:52.588
Where in 1939 there were 10 cases of tuberculosis,

00:02:52.989 --> 00:02:54.491
There are 15 of them today.

00:02:55.111 --> 00:02:56.573
But to heal our children,

00:02:57.234 --> 00:03:01.158
Beds will be needed in the beautiful countries where the joy of living is rediscovered in the modern era...

00:03:02.678 --> 00:03:04.418
The great disease of the time,

00:03:04.679 --> 00:03:05.659
It was tuberculosis.

00:03:05.661 --> 00:03:07.460
We had nothing to treat her with,

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:13.362
simply to put people in the sun and to rest.

00:03:14.203 --> 00:03:17.464
Since this disease was, in fact, quite contagious,

00:03:18.044 --> 00:03:18.685
by spitting,

00:03:18.725 --> 00:03:19.925
by so many things.

00:03:20.065 --> 00:03:21.086
But we had nothing at the time.

00:03:21.626 --> 00:03:23.367
So that's why...

00:03:24.908 --> 00:03:27.349
that is to say, immediately after the war of 1914,

00:03:27.729 --> 00:03:31.311
The mayor considered building a large complex.

00:03:31.751 --> 00:03:32.912
something immense,

00:03:33.532 --> 00:03:37.313
and they chose this wood which was called the wood of the mute woman.

00:03:54.816 --> 00:03:56.076
The initial work,

00:03:56.177 --> 00:03:58.237
It's going to be 1921.

00:03:58.239 --> 00:03:59.139
and then little by little,

00:03:59.140 --> 00:03:59.659
gradually,

00:03:59.659 --> 00:04:01.260
We started with a display stand,

00:04:01.439 --> 00:04:03.081
then a number of buildings,

00:04:04.122 --> 00:04:05.302
huge buildings,

00:04:05.383 --> 00:04:07.645
since there are three rows,

00:04:07.705 --> 00:04:08.365
if I may say so,

00:04:08.905 --> 00:04:09.405
and of which...

00:04:09.705 --> 00:04:14.287
over 370 meters long and barely 10 meters wide,

00:04:14.326 --> 00:04:22.088
because you only had rooms with good southern exposure and a corridor to go from room to room with,

00:04:22.288 --> 00:04:22.889
Of course,

00:04:23.528 --> 00:04:27.310
a number of other outbuildings,

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:28.509
if I may say so.

00:04:29.990 --> 00:04:32.230
I'm sending you a photo of our new home.

00:04:33.151 --> 00:04:36.091
Those were women who were there when the photo was taken.

00:04:36.891 --> 00:04:37.391
because before,

00:04:37.752 --> 00:04:38.851
It was a rest home.

00:04:39.732 --> 00:04:40.232
NOW,

00:04:40.473 --> 00:04:41.374
It's a sanatorium.

00:04:42.855 --> 00:04:44.137
My room is marked with a cross.

00:04:44.617 --> 00:04:45.598
and the bed you see,

00:04:46.139 --> 00:04:46.639
It's mine.

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:48.861
There are two women per room.

00:04:49.442 --> 00:04:52.064
and I have a radio to keep me entertained.

00:05:08.722 --> 00:05:15.526
How sad!

00:05:15.747 --> 00:05:17.548
We used to come here in the 1950s.

00:05:17.627 --> 00:05:18.629
Oh yes,

00:05:18.769 --> 00:05:20.129
back then I was very young,

00:05:20.189 --> 00:05:20.850
I was...

00:05:20.870 --> 00:05:22.172
Well, we weren't very tall either.

00:05:23.593 --> 00:05:25.413
We were still in kindergarten with Jean-Luc.

00:05:25.454 --> 00:05:25.954
Yeah...

00:05:28.019 --> 00:05:30.320
My father was the city architect.

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:33.181
My dad won the competition.

00:05:34.923 --> 00:05:35.423
Because in fact,

00:05:35.423 --> 00:05:39.484
There had been a competition for the design of the sanatorium.

00:05:39.964 --> 00:05:40.524
And so,

00:05:40.584 --> 00:05:41.945
the mayor at the time,

00:05:42.045 --> 00:05:43.045
Maurice Violette,

00:05:45.486 --> 00:05:47.127
He trusted him.

00:05:47.967 --> 00:05:52.569
And he was the one who designed the entire preventorium building.

00:05:52.649 --> 00:05:53.610
of the sanatorium.

00:05:57.067 --> 00:06:00.512
That is to say, there was still a demand for

00:06:01.172 --> 00:06:02.673
X rooms.

00:06:02.733 --> 00:06:03.233
SO,

00:06:03.855 --> 00:06:04.956
rather than doing a...

00:06:05.776 --> 00:06:08.059
He preferred to build different types of buildings.

00:06:08.860 --> 00:06:09.620
an alignment,

00:06:09.622 --> 00:06:11.101
Finally, a composition.

00:06:12.324 --> 00:06:12.644
So, there,

00:06:12.644 --> 00:06:15.826
It was in his mind that the idea started to take shape.

00:06:32.504 --> 00:06:33.465
I was very proud of it.

00:06:33.504 --> 00:06:34.706
I was very proud of my dad.

00:06:34.867 --> 00:06:37.030
Yet we didn't actually know each other.

00:06:37.290 --> 00:06:41.016
Because he worked from midnight until 4 a.m.,

00:06:41.256 --> 00:06:41.937
He was waking up,

00:06:42.016 --> 00:06:48.826
He would have his little coffee in his bathroom, which my mother had prepared for him the day before, and that was that.

00:06:57.336 --> 00:07:00.158
When you look at the architect's plans,

00:07:00.578 --> 00:07:03.180
He often draws a shape resembling wind.

00:07:03.281 --> 00:07:04.641
as if the wind were rushing in.

00:07:05.002 --> 00:07:06.203
Because in order to treat the sick,

00:07:06.263 --> 00:07:07.403
We also needed fresh air.

00:07:07.944 --> 00:07:09.884
And so we often see all the...

00:07:10.365 --> 00:07:10.826
Finally,
the wind,

00:07:10.826 --> 00:07:11.627
He draws it like this.

00:07:11.907 --> 00:07:14.908
If you zoom in on these shots,

00:07:14.968 --> 00:07:15.370
and in fact,

00:07:15.370 --> 00:07:16.269
I imagined it like that,

00:07:16.290 --> 00:07:18.091
like a wind pattern that would come...

00:07:18.792 --> 00:07:19.151
But anyway,

00:07:19.151 --> 00:07:23.175
This is a personal interpretation and not necessarily the truth.

00:07:33.176 --> 00:07:34.896
What I also liked about those balconies,

00:07:35.538 --> 00:07:36.497
It was the little details.

00:07:37.818 --> 00:07:39.538
Are there still places where we can climb or not?

00:07:39.539 --> 00:07:41.779
Because I see that quite a few shutters have fallen.

00:07:48.423 --> 00:07:50.283
It's so great to be here after all this time.

00:07:50.284 --> 00:07:52.224
It's beautiful nonetheless.

00:08:01.340 --> 00:08:01.904
Which is very funny,

00:08:01.905 --> 00:08:03.676
It's that in the organization of the buildings...

00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:04.939
What I find interesting,

00:08:04.941 --> 00:08:05.819
that's the very first one,

00:08:06.120 --> 00:08:07.120
the one in front,

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:09.221
truly towards the grand landscape

00:08:10.401 --> 00:08:11.942
It was the pavilion of people who had money.

00:08:13.021 --> 00:08:14.562
It's really very well done.

00:08:14.942 --> 00:08:15.244
Afterwards,

00:08:15.244 --> 00:08:15.983
there was the pavilion,

00:08:16.143 --> 00:08:16.682
the second,

00:08:17.083 --> 00:08:17.583
who was,

00:08:18.064 --> 00:08:19.163
I believe it was the poor.

00:08:19.304 --> 00:08:21.024
those who didn't necessarily have the means to pay,

00:08:21.064 --> 00:08:22.345
so those who were not on the front line.

00:08:22.485 --> 00:08:23.625
There was none,

00:08:23.665 --> 00:08:24.725
them,
the wide, unobstructed view.

00:08:25.406 --> 00:08:25.805
And then,

00:08:25.805 --> 00:08:26.545
That's very cynical.

00:08:26.547 --> 00:08:26.947
But then,

00:08:26.947 --> 00:08:27.466
the last one,

00:08:27.946 --> 00:08:29.687
It was the one for the very, very sick.

00:08:30.507 --> 00:08:32.608
Later,
the children's pavilion was added in the second phase.

00:08:33.048 --> 00:08:34.087
But those who were very...

00:08:34.147 --> 00:08:36.888
sick and right behind was the crematorium.

00:08:36.908 --> 00:08:40.570
In fact, it's the modernism plan.

00:08:41.490 --> 00:08:47.033
In the foreground are those who had money, then we move further away, and the sicker you are, the more you go towards the crematorium, you know.

00:08:48.373 --> 00:08:48.873
The shape,

00:08:48.993 --> 00:08:49.674
the function there,

00:08:50.174 --> 00:08:50.674
We're there.

00:08:55.296 --> 00:09:01.019
I graduated from Paris-Malaquais, completing my diploma at the Dreux sanitarium.

00:09:02.572 --> 00:09:08.576
And so I found this one because it combined quite a few criteria that I found interesting.

00:09:09.257 --> 00:09:11.218
The entire aspect of the modernist plan,

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:12.500
etc.

00:09:12.820 --> 00:09:16.043
But also on all those details of 1930s Art Deco,

00:09:16.803 --> 00:09:17.784
which I liked very much,

00:09:18.305 --> 00:09:20.126
It's an era that I really appreciate.

00:09:20.627 --> 00:09:24.410
And there are plenty of details that we don't necessarily pay attention to.

00:09:24.570 --> 00:09:28.735
But it's going to be all these little forms of wind,

00:09:28.855 --> 00:09:30.035
the wind rushing in.

00:09:30.844 --> 00:09:32.445
At the level of the facades,

00:09:32.446 --> 00:09:33.446
We're going to look at some small details.

00:09:33.466 --> 00:09:43.653
We have railings that are very typical of the 1930s and especially the letters on the pavilions which are very typical and which I found very interesting.

00:09:44.634 --> 00:09:46.215
That was the cover of my dissertation.

00:09:46.216 --> 00:09:57.402
What interested me and the aspect I worked on,

00:09:57.582 --> 00:09:59.543
It was programmatic obsolescence.

00:10:00.128 --> 00:10:02.589
That is to say, sanatoriums,

00:10:02.629 --> 00:10:03.629
like other buildings,

00:10:03.789 --> 00:10:06.792
are built for a specific purpose.

00:10:07.572 --> 00:10:08.832
That's the genius of these buildings,

00:10:08.932 --> 00:10:11.653
That's because we build them to meet a need.

00:10:12.014 --> 00:10:14.995
Here,
it was about defeating tuberculosis when vaccines didn't exist.

00:10:15.396 --> 00:10:15.716
And so,

00:10:15.716 --> 00:10:17.517
These are buildings with very unusual shapes.

00:10:19.437 --> 00:10:21.560
which at least made it possible to treat the sick.

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:23.300
So,
it was in the open air,

00:10:23.360 --> 00:10:24.401
exposure to light,

00:10:24.402 --> 00:10:24.902
etc.

00:10:25.682 --> 00:10:26.981
And so,
these are buildings which,

00:10:27.983 --> 00:10:29.724
due to their typological aspect,

00:10:30.507 --> 00:10:33.089
They are very difficult to maintain today.

00:10:33.129 --> 00:10:33.750
to renovate,

00:10:34.650 --> 00:10:37.651
since the vaccines were discovered,

00:10:37.851 --> 00:10:42.314
and also that we no longer have any use for those buildings.

00:10:43.274 --> 00:10:43.775
And so,

00:10:43.835 --> 00:10:45.235
They are left to decay.

00:10:45.275 --> 00:10:45.856
and for some,

00:10:45.876 --> 00:10:47.517
for many years.

00:11:30.677 --> 00:11:31.177
Alas,

00:11:31.597 --> 00:11:32.717
Hitler is hungry.

00:11:33.538 --> 00:11:35.659
During an election campaign a year ago,

00:11:36.384 --> 00:11:37.384
We warned you,

00:11:37.485 --> 00:11:37.985
friends,

00:11:38.004 --> 00:11:40.067
that there was nothing to be done with fascism.

00:11:40.846 --> 00:11:42.888
However, you wanted to trust him.

00:11:43.288 --> 00:11:44.549
We were warmongers.

00:11:45.350 --> 00:11:45.850
Today,

00:11:45.931 --> 00:11:46.650
You can see that clearly,

00:11:46.711 --> 00:11:48.552
Hitler will always be hungry.

00:11:50.494 --> 00:11:51.193
Maurice Violette,

00:11:51.194 --> 00:11:52.975
August 30, 1939

00:12:11.203 --> 00:12:15.925
When the Germans approached Dreux in June 1940,

00:12:16.725 --> 00:12:19.226
the director of the sanatorium,

00:12:19.927 --> 00:12:21.067
a baradier doctor,

00:12:22.008 --> 00:12:22.808
has run away,

00:12:22.848 --> 00:12:23.808
shouting "every man for himself",

00:12:23.848 --> 00:12:24.609
And he disappeared.

00:12:25.088 --> 00:12:28.890
leaving his patients at the sanatorium stranded.

00:12:32.032 --> 00:12:34.493
And so we called my stepfather in a panic.

00:12:35.313 --> 00:12:41.716
And that is why he arrived in Dreux very quickly and settled in the doctor's house.

00:12:42.497 --> 00:12:43.697
I don't know if it was that one.

00:12:43.737 --> 00:12:45.018
That was the administration.

00:12:45.418 --> 00:12:46.718
I think his offices were there.

00:12:48.839 --> 00:12:50.780
During the Second World War,

00:12:51.400 --> 00:12:53.261
The Germans were really scared,

00:12:53.301 --> 00:12:54.042
if I may say so

00:12:54.322 --> 00:12:59.304
The chances of getting sick and catching tuberculosis were not too high.

00:13:00.639 --> 00:13:01.480
of these buildings,

00:13:01.721 --> 00:13:08.685
This allowed Dr. Roche to be able to hide Jews or resistance fighters.

00:13:11.166 --> 00:13:18.310
Dr. Roche and his wife altered patients' X-rays at night and falsified test results.

00:13:18.409 --> 00:13:18.930
Well, that's it.

00:13:19.471 --> 00:13:20.250
when I learned about it,

00:13:20.251 --> 00:13:21.451
It really...

00:13:23.212 --> 00:13:24.873
There were obviously sick people too,

00:13:25.494 --> 00:13:28.654
I was going to say there were real sick people in that sanatorium.

00:13:28.875 --> 00:13:29.416
obviously.

00:13:30.104 --> 00:13:33.365
But at the time, we were analyzing spit.

00:13:34.345 --> 00:13:41.207
And so my stepfather changed the name on the samples that were taken from the patients.

00:13:41.687 --> 00:13:49.490
And when the Germans demanded proof as to whether the Jews were actually sick,

00:13:49.509 --> 00:13:51.451
Jewish doctors in particular,

00:13:51.451 --> 00:13:52.671
I'm thinking of Dr. Edner.

00:13:53.331 --> 00:13:55.052
because my stepfather had talked to me about it a lot.

00:14:02.944 --> 00:14:03.444
A day,

00:14:03.444 --> 00:14:05.085
I was resting,

00:14:05.825 --> 00:14:07.525
And I was still worried.

00:14:08.004 --> 00:14:09.786
I knew that at any moment,

00:14:10.006 --> 00:14:11.126
The weapons could arrive.

00:14:11.646 --> 00:14:12.807
And I heard "boom,

00:14:13.247 --> 00:14:13.747
boom.

00:14:14.527 --> 00:14:16.728
And I knew,

00:14:16.828 --> 00:14:17.908
I was trying on some Germans.

00:14:18.208 --> 00:14:20.289
They came looking for me.

00:14:20.469 --> 00:14:20.989
another.

00:14:21.209 --> 00:14:21.849
There was another one,

00:14:21.869 --> 00:14:23.010
I was hiding there.

00:14:23.890 --> 00:14:24.390
And then,

00:14:24.390 --> 00:14:24.890
I have some...

00:14:26.290 --> 00:14:27.131
“Dr. Hegner,

00:14:27.230 --> 00:14:29.291
"Yes?" I told him, "Look,

00:14:29.411 --> 00:14:30.472
"Pack your suitcase"

00:14:30.492 --> 00:14:31.592
And he made a phone call.

00:14:32.460 --> 00:14:33.240
At the Gestapo,

00:14:33.480 --> 00:14:35.121
he said, but there,

00:14:36.562 --> 00:14:39.105
the doctor we had gone to get,

00:14:39.585 --> 00:14:40.446
He has tuberculosis.

00:14:40.665 --> 00:14:41.606
It is contagious.

00:14:44.928 --> 00:14:47.091
He was able to stay in the sanatorium.

00:14:47.490 --> 00:14:51.073
The Germans came back for him a second time.

00:14:53.715 --> 00:14:54.296
Again,

00:14:55.155 --> 00:15:00.000
The fact that he was always positive is what made him come back.

00:15:00.028 --> 00:15:01.428
He went back to the sanatorium.

00:15:01.489 --> 00:15:02.389
Moreover, it's also dry.

00:15:02.750 --> 00:15:03.250
and there,

00:15:03.350 --> 00:15:07.194
That's when he remembered an acquaintance.

00:15:07.433 --> 00:15:08.215
where he crossed,

00:15:08.634 --> 00:15:09.896
Someone came to visit,

00:15:11.837 --> 00:15:13.820
a lady he had cared for in the past,

00:15:14.080 --> 00:15:16.981
and he asked her if he could disappear to her house.

00:15:24.769 --> 00:15:26.570
They never stated this in our presence.

00:15:26.651 --> 00:15:27.551
in front of their children

00:15:27.552 --> 00:15:28.351
of their activities.

00:15:28.552 --> 00:15:32.452
that they had in a resistance network called Libération Nord.

00:15:33.312 --> 00:15:34.494
And in fact,

00:15:34.774 --> 00:15:36.134
This is Dr. Jean Baudvinic.

00:15:36.333 --> 00:15:37.294
a doctor from Dreux,

00:15:38.014 --> 00:15:43.836
who discovered this because he was searching for people who had helped Jews during the war.

00:15:44.776 --> 00:15:47.038
And that's absolutely incredible.

00:15:47.057 --> 00:15:50.198
Finally,
it was an absolutely incredible discovery for the whole family,

00:15:51.139 --> 00:15:51.838
for their children

00:15:51.858 --> 00:15:52.918
for their grandchildren,

00:15:53.019 --> 00:15:54.720
and especially since they had disappeared.

00:15:55.495 --> 00:15:57.317
And that was the only thing we knew,

00:15:57.476 --> 00:16:01.759
That's what my mother-in-law told me, saying,

00:16:02.479 --> 00:16:05.539
Yes, indeed, during the war radios were falsified.

00:16:05.600 --> 00:16:07.620
We changed the analyses.

00:16:07.621 --> 00:16:08.360
It was very...

00:16:09.201 --> 00:16:09.841
She saw that,

00:16:10.621 --> 00:16:11.862
I wouldn't say it was just for fun.

00:16:11.864 --> 00:16:12.783
but it gave him,

00:16:12.903 --> 00:16:15.903
It certainly gave them both a great deal of satisfaction.

00:16:24.687 --> 00:16:27.809
That's impressive.

00:16:27.811 --> 00:16:28.551
As you can see,

00:16:28.591 --> 00:16:30.292
This place is absolutely huge.

00:16:31.673 --> 00:16:36.856
Many staff members were very attentive in order to do everything they could.

00:16:36.876 --> 00:16:41.080
obviously to protect the sick and the Jews, naturally.

00:16:46.462 --> 00:16:51.047
There must have been at least sixty people working for the children.

00:16:53.536 --> 00:16:54.436
At the sanatorium,

00:16:54.536 --> 00:16:58.197
There must have been two or three hundred people working for the sanatorium.

00:16:58.798 --> 00:17:00.378
Among these two or three hundred people,

00:17:00.418 --> 00:17:02.139
There were resistance fighters.

00:17:03.079 --> 00:17:03.918
Who were they?

00:17:04.439 --> 00:17:05.279
We don't really know.

00:17:06.380 --> 00:17:08.120
What was their relationship with each other?

00:17:08.921 --> 00:17:10.081
We don't really know either.

00:17:11.461 --> 00:17:16.263
But of course there were cells that were not necessarily in contact with each other.

00:17:16.403 --> 00:17:17.823
And that was also the code of silence.

00:17:17.824 --> 00:17:18.903
And we knew about it.

00:17:18.904 --> 00:17:19.703
And the better it was.

00:17:26.683 --> 00:17:27.163
Finally,

00:17:27.163 --> 00:17:27.803
if we can say so,

00:17:27.824 --> 00:17:30.865
There were a few people who benefited from this welcome,

00:17:31.144 --> 00:17:36.547
but we cannot say that there were many or not many.

00:17:37.646 --> 00:17:39.927
The only traces to truly speak,

00:17:41.067 --> 00:17:42.788
historically speaking and in reality,

00:17:43.209 --> 00:17:45.349
We need to have written records somewhere.

00:17:46.190 --> 00:17:47.990
even testimonies, possibly

00:17:48.370 --> 00:17:52.531
but real testimonies from people who have experienced this, and not just rumors.

00:17:54.197 --> 00:17:57.622
I heard that 300 Jewish children were saved at the Preventorium.

00:17:57.682 --> 00:17:58.583
That's completely false.

00:18:00.045 --> 00:18:02.749
There were indeed 300 children at the time of the collapse,

00:18:03.210 --> 00:18:06.433
led by the two schoolteachers when the Germans arrived,

00:18:06.454 --> 00:18:10.620
but there were never 300 Jewish children at the Preventorium.

00:18:32.688 --> 00:18:33.609
We lived in Dreux.

00:18:40.315 --> 00:18:43.076
I started having breathing problems.

00:18:44.178 --> 00:18:45.019
We went to the doctor.

00:18:45.039 --> 00:18:45.680
they told us,

00:18:45.860 --> 00:18:47.221
You have tuberculosis.

00:18:47.421 --> 00:18:49.543
We need to send you to the sanatorium in Dreux.

00:18:50.955 --> 00:18:55.638
Then I unfortunately infected my brother,

00:18:55.938 --> 00:18:56.458
my sister,

00:18:57.059 --> 00:18:59.300
And the three of us ended up here.

00:19:00.201 --> 00:19:04.463
Me with my brother and sister with the girls.

00:19:07.245 --> 00:19:08.126
And now, do you remember what that was or not?

00:19:08.127 --> 00:19:09.206
Oh no, I don't know what that was.

00:19:09.406 --> 00:19:10.827
Well, we were only there once.

00:19:11.107 --> 00:19:12.848
We passed through once in one direction.

00:19:12.908 --> 00:19:13.649
once in the other.

00:19:14.189 --> 00:19:15.610
That's what we're doing today.

00:19:24.271 --> 00:19:25.732
There was an operating room,

00:19:25.792 --> 00:19:27.094
but perhaps there,

00:19:27.173 --> 00:19:27.673
I wasn't.

00:19:28.934 --> 00:19:30.034
The refectory was there.

00:19:35.478 --> 00:19:37.158
There,
were the spa treatments.

00:19:40.921 --> 00:19:42.001
There,
it was the dormitory world.

00:19:48.105 --> 00:19:49.145
Come on,
the slur was there.

00:19:50.567 --> 00:19:51.386
We were passing by there,

00:19:51.787 --> 00:19:52.988
and we would go to the shower.

00:19:54.085 --> 00:20:00.000
It reminds me of the veterans right now who are visiting the sites where they landed 80 years ago.

00:20:00.532 --> 00:20:01.974
I feel like I'm doing the same as them.

00:20:02.214 --> 00:20:03.015
that I return,

00:20:03.415 --> 00:20:05.617
but not on a war site.

00:20:12.363 --> 00:20:15.686
I unfortunately returned here in 1951.

00:20:15.687 --> 00:20:16.187
in October,

00:20:16.727 --> 00:20:18.509
and I got out a year later.

00:20:18.749 --> 00:20:22.973
in October 1952.

00:20:22.974 --> 00:20:26.396
There were about 35 of us children.

00:20:27.243 --> 00:20:27.743
Of

00:20:28.403 --> 00:20:29.164
8 years at

00:20:29.504 --> 00:20:31.525
15 years old,

00:20:32.025 --> 00:20:33.306
I was the oldest one, by the way.

00:20:34.006 --> 00:20:36.487
I was too young to go to the men's ward,

00:20:37.146 --> 00:20:42.429
and I could only go to the children.

00:20:44.128 --> 00:20:45.128
When I first arrived,

00:20:45.189 --> 00:20:46.529
There were about forty of us.

00:20:46.569 --> 00:20:47.049
no more.

00:20:47.049 --> 00:20:47.569
maximum.

00:20:48.049 --> 00:20:48.769
And then,

00:20:49.050 --> 00:20:50.530
Some have found themselves cured.

00:20:51.070 --> 00:20:51.810
When I went out,

00:20:51.850 --> 00:20:53.751
There were only 25 or 30 of us left.

00:20:54.347 --> 00:20:55.107
The first day,

00:20:55.167 --> 00:20:56.088
when we arrived here,

00:20:56.168 --> 00:20:57.229
When I told you at the entrance,

00:20:57.230 --> 00:20:59.448
It was marked "entered on such and such a day".

00:21:00.169 --> 00:21:02.230
died on such and such a day.

00:21:03.109 --> 00:21:04.931
It didn't happen like that for me.

00:21:07.192 --> 00:21:08.192
When we arrived here,

00:21:08.271 --> 00:21:12.212
We were first given a sputum aspiration to examine the sputum.

00:21:12.213 --> 00:21:14.032
if we were still infected and all that.

00:21:14.052 --> 00:21:14.453
And then,

00:21:14.453 --> 00:21:15.573
We were given treatment.

00:21:16.594 --> 00:21:19.375
It was pasignacide and rémifond,

00:21:19.835 --> 00:21:20.634
which still exist

00:21:20.674 --> 00:21:21.174
I believe.

00:21:21.995 --> 00:21:23.235
and then...

00:21:23.960 --> 00:21:25.581
Since I wasn't healing quickly,

00:21:25.582 --> 00:21:26.721
I had a pneumothorax.

00:21:28.603 --> 00:21:30.064
So, pneumothorax,

00:21:30.183 --> 00:21:33.885
Air is insufflated between the pleura and the lung.

00:21:34.066 --> 00:21:38.249
in order to shrink the affected lung.

00:21:38.869 --> 00:21:39.830
I had injuries,

00:21:39.890 --> 00:21:40.789
I had two lesions,

00:21:41.431 --> 00:21:45.753
So I had the pneumothorax for almost two and a half years.

00:21:46.834 --> 00:21:47.714
Once I got out,

00:21:47.734 --> 00:21:50.615
I was getting breaths at the dispensary in Dreux.

00:21:52.084 --> 00:21:52.943
Every two weeks,

00:21:52.944 --> 00:21:58.046
We needed to be given more air so that our lungs would remain contracted.

00:21:58.965 --> 00:22:00.386
so that it can heal.

00:22:04.488 --> 00:22:05.827
It was a dormitory,

00:22:06.367 --> 00:22:07.489
There were 40 beds,

00:22:08.929 --> 00:22:11.089
There were baffles between each bed,

00:22:11.090 --> 00:22:12.710
small squeaky toys.

00:22:12.990 --> 00:22:13.630
There,
at the beginning,

00:22:13.810 --> 00:22:14.371
when I arrived,

00:22:14.431 --> 00:22:16.171
There were 38 of us.

00:22:16.230 --> 00:22:16.932
something like that,

00:22:16.971 --> 00:22:18.072
with an adobre.

00:22:19.415 --> 00:22:20.576
And in the morning,

00:22:20.696 --> 00:22:21.716
There was the toilet,

00:22:22.596 --> 00:22:23.917
then breakfast.

00:22:24.197 --> 00:22:25.658
So there was a refectory,

00:22:26.238 --> 00:22:27.078
The girls on one side,

00:22:27.178 --> 00:22:28.519
the boys on the other side.

00:22:29.379 --> 00:22:31.560
All this under the supervision of Sister Maria.

00:22:32.240 --> 00:22:35.163
And there were five maids.

00:22:39.083 --> 00:22:40.224
There was already a school.

00:22:40.664 --> 00:22:41.904
There was a school for girls,

00:22:41.984 --> 00:22:45.466
Finally, a classroom for girls on one side and boys on the other.

00:22:45.807 --> 00:22:46.807
which was run by Mr.

00:22:46.947 --> 00:22:47.867
and Mrs. Dubost.

00:22:53.840 --> 00:22:54.860
After that came the treatments,

00:22:55.621 --> 00:22:58.821
Afterwards there was lunch and then we went back to the spa.

00:22:58.822 --> 00:23:02.162
So you had to stay lying on your back,

00:23:02.983 --> 00:23:03.963
to do nothing.

00:23:04.923 --> 00:23:05.623
One could read,

00:23:05.643 --> 00:23:06.263
things like that,

00:23:06.323 --> 00:23:07.224
But we mustn't move.

00:23:07.225 --> 00:23:09.084
We mustn't move.

00:23:09.085 --> 00:23:11.185
You should stay on your back as much as possible.

00:23:11.186 --> 00:23:12.705
What were you reading?

00:23:12.706 --> 00:23:14.085
We weren't allowed to read.

00:23:14.086 --> 00:23:14.986
I don't remember anymore.

00:23:15.506 --> 00:23:17.206
At that time, people read Tintin comics,

00:23:17.766 --> 00:23:18.387
Spirou,

00:23:18.567 --> 00:23:19.247
Things like that.

00:23:19.367 --> 00:23:23.008
It wasn't the sister who lent us books.

00:23:44.398 --> 00:23:45.399
At
the age of 4,

00:23:46.299 --> 00:23:50.222
I was diagnosed with a fairly significant primary infection.

00:23:51.864 --> 00:23:53.284
So at the time,

00:23:53.305 --> 00:23:55.026
There were quite a few people with tuberculosis.

00:23:58.832 --> 00:23:59.573
Places like this,

00:23:59.773 --> 00:24:00.514
immense,

00:24:00.673 --> 00:24:01.835
pretty much everywhere in France.

00:24:02.075 --> 00:24:02.915
And me,

00:24:02.956 --> 00:24:05.718
I was put in this sanatorium.

00:24:11.531 --> 00:24:12.192
Because people,

00:24:13.012 --> 00:24:14.394
They didn't want to come near us.

00:24:14.594 --> 00:24:15.714
because it's very contagious.

00:24:16.276 --> 00:24:18.797
If you send spittle to someone,

00:24:18.998 --> 00:24:19.998
that you have tuberculosis.

00:24:20.898 --> 00:24:21.900
We talked about Covid,

00:24:22.359 --> 00:24:24.961
But that was huge at the time.

00:24:25.461 --> 00:24:27.544
I have several aunts who died at 17.

00:24:28.605 --> 00:24:29.965
And all the families,

00:24:30.425 --> 00:24:31.067
rich or poor,

00:24:31.146 --> 00:24:32.267
There were tuberculosis patients.

00:24:42.123 --> 00:24:43.164
To get me to come here,

00:24:44.986 --> 00:24:49.950
My father told me a story, saying that we were going into the forest,

00:24:50.471 --> 00:24:51.311
in a beautiful location

00:24:51.932 --> 00:24:52.692
to see deer.

00:24:53.934 --> 00:24:56.316
We might bring one home,

00:24:56.996 --> 00:24:59.178
especially in a Parisian apartment

00:24:59.318 --> 00:24:59.999
That would have been pretty good.

00:25:00.028 --> 00:25:00.528
That's not bad.

00:25:00.729 --> 00:25:01.229
I think so.

00:25:02.870 --> 00:25:03.370
And then,

00:25:03.631 --> 00:25:04.010
SO,

00:25:04.010 --> 00:25:04.810
I arrived here.

00:25:05.412 --> 00:25:05.731
And then,

00:25:05.731 --> 00:25:06.231
Of course,

00:25:06.231 --> 00:25:07.894
I realized I was going to stay there.

00:25:14.739 --> 00:25:17.601
There was a little girl who was very pale,

00:25:17.980 --> 00:25:19.322
who must have been 8 years old,

00:25:19.323 --> 00:25:19.823
maybe.

00:25:21.043 --> 00:25:21.543
And then,

00:25:22.024 --> 00:25:22.743
I approached,

00:25:22.784 --> 00:25:23.565
But we didn't speak to each other.

00:25:24.245 --> 00:25:25.286
She always did that to me.

00:25:26.146 --> 00:25:27.347
And I,
I sat down on the ground.

00:25:28.028 --> 00:25:30.028
And then we looked at each other like that,

00:25:31.110 --> 00:25:31.710
I loved it.

00:25:31.831 --> 00:25:32.811
I thought she was beautiful.

00:25:33.332 --> 00:25:34.152
like a little elf.

00:25:35.773 --> 00:25:36.374
Then I thought to myself,

00:25:36.375 --> 00:25:40.937
I might still go see Bambi, closer to her.

00:25:40.938 --> 00:25:42.356
or things like that.

00:25:42.417 --> 00:25:43.698
I have a lot of imagination.

00:25:45.399 --> 00:25:45.881
And then one day,

00:25:45.881 --> 00:25:46.420
I haven't seen it since.

00:25:48.301 --> 00:25:51.624
And besides, I was still keeping my ear to the ground.

00:25:52.520 --> 00:25:55.382
I hear instructors saying, "Oh well, you know,

00:25:55.423 --> 00:25:55.923
the little one... »

00:25:56.303 --> 00:25:57.684
I don't know what they called him next door.

00:25:57.704 --> 00:25:59.546
"She left last night."

00:26:01.868 --> 00:26:02.408
Without understanding,

00:26:02.428 --> 00:26:04.950
I did understand, though, that I wouldn't see her again.

00:26:11.176 --> 00:26:11.676
The morning,

00:26:12.397 --> 00:26:14.879
We had a café au lait or a kind of hot chocolate with milk.

00:26:16.060 --> 00:26:17.381
but instead of bowls,

00:26:17.521 --> 00:26:20.543
They were like the bowls we give to dogs now.

00:26:21.683 --> 00:26:22.925
So, a little girl,

00:26:23.525 --> 00:26:24.226
How do you want it?

00:26:24.706 --> 00:26:26.287
then either with a tablespoon,

00:26:27.008 --> 00:26:29.810
Or else you had to take the bowl and do that.

00:26:30.791 --> 00:26:32.312
And then we had slices of bread and butter.

00:26:34.692 --> 00:26:35.913
And then there were large ones,

00:26:36.595 --> 00:26:40.978
We often put smaller ones next to larger ones so that the larger ones could take care of them.

00:26:42.019 --> 00:26:42.558
And the big ones,

00:26:42.560 --> 00:26:45.401
Sometimes,
they would steal our sandwiches.

00:26:46.542 --> 00:26:47.502
And on Sundays,

00:26:47.942 --> 00:26:49.264
So we had visitors.

00:26:50.305 --> 00:26:54.528
We were given less to eat because they knew that the parents brought treats.

00:26:55.648 --> 00:26:56.549
And then in the evening,

00:26:57.089 --> 00:26:57.691
every Sunday,

00:26:57.711 --> 00:26:58.671
It was the same thing.

00:26:59.432 --> 00:27:00.913
They gave us pudding.

00:27:01.614 --> 00:27:02.114
And then, this...

00:27:02.114 --> 00:27:03.075
I loved it though.

00:27:03.194 --> 00:27:04.316
But that's all we had to eat.

00:27:05.237 --> 00:27:05.737
supposedly,

00:27:05.777 --> 00:27:08.199
because the parents had brought sweets.

00:27:09.579 --> 00:27:10.079
Dear Auntie,

00:27:10.480 --> 00:27:10.980
Dear Uncle,

00:27:11.961 --> 00:27:12.461
I'm doing well.

00:27:13.143 --> 00:27:13.743
I eat well.

00:27:14.423 --> 00:27:15.464
I think you're doing well.

00:27:16.205 --> 00:27:17.586
We went to the cinema on Sunday.

00:27:18.307 --> 00:27:19.087
I'm resting well.

00:27:19.688 --> 00:27:20.788
We build wooden cabins.

00:27:21.470 --> 00:27:22.411
I am in good health.

00:27:23.051 --> 00:27:23.832
I send you my warmest regards.

00:27:25.413 --> 00:27:26.314
So,
the essence of cinema,

00:27:26.315 --> 00:27:26.815
I loved it.

00:27:26.894 --> 00:27:27.375
Of course,

00:27:27.375 --> 00:27:28.556
but it was all in black and white.

00:27:29.417 --> 00:27:29.817
SO,

00:27:29.817 --> 00:27:30.317
as a film,

00:27:30.317 --> 00:27:32.080
They were often rather sad films.

00:27:32.620 --> 00:27:34.061
So,
there were the two orphans,

00:27:34.121 --> 00:27:34.981
I don't remember whose it is.

00:27:35.021 --> 00:27:35.343
That.

00:27:35.343 --> 00:27:35.843
Anyway,

00:27:36.123 --> 00:27:36.442
But anyway,

00:27:36.442 --> 00:27:36.942
That makes me...

00:27:38.205 --> 00:27:40.687
And the film that made everyone laugh,

00:27:40.928 --> 00:27:41.428
it was

00:27:41.688 --> 00:27:43.190
François I with Fernandelle.

00:27:44.371 --> 00:27:45.751
So,
there was a passage.

00:27:46.167 --> 00:27:46.928
Phew,
Ferdandelle,

00:27:47.188 --> 00:27:47.809
He is lying down.

00:27:48.190 --> 00:27:49.110
We're going to torture him.

00:27:49.871 --> 00:27:50.591
That's a joke.

00:27:50.871 --> 00:27:51.572
What.

00:27:51.752 --> 00:27:56.897
They put salt on his feet and a goat licks his feet.

00:27:57.759 --> 00:28:00.621
She's going to make him...

00:28:00.623 --> 00:28:01.461
For me,
it wasn't funny.

00:28:01.643 --> 00:28:03.023
I had the impression that he was suffering.

00:28:11.299 --> 00:28:13.600
Here is the X-ray image of the boy.

00:28:13.840 --> 00:28:17.042
Unfortunately, this confirms the previously established diagnosis.

00:28:17.083 --> 00:28:18.522
The child has tuberculosis.

00:28:18.923 --> 00:28:20.963
His good health is only apparent.

00:28:21.384 --> 00:28:25.026
This round cavern is the typical lesion of childhood tuberculosis.

00:28:31.689 --> 00:28:32.710
Well,
radio is nothing.

00:28:33.329 --> 00:28:33.829
Breathe,

00:28:33.829 --> 00:28:34.490
Breathe more.

00:28:36.171 --> 00:28:37.331
Because we had tuberculosis.

00:28:37.975 --> 00:28:39.837
Regular testing was necessary.

00:28:39.917 --> 00:28:40.417
And then,

00:28:40.518 --> 00:28:42.839
They're doing a tubing analysis for you.

00:28:43.280 --> 00:28:43.880
At the time,

00:28:44.000 --> 00:28:44.741
when I was 4 years old,

00:28:44.742 --> 00:28:45.762
the pipe was like this.

00:28:47.624 --> 00:28:49.605
And they pinched my nose.

00:28:50.807 --> 00:28:52.268
And then you had to open your mouth.

00:28:52.588 --> 00:28:56.831
and the tube passed all the way to the stomach to collect the mucus.

00:28:57.252 --> 00:28:57.673
Mucus,

00:28:57.673 --> 00:28:59.954
We analyze them to see if there are still what are called BKs present.

00:29:01.675 --> 00:29:02.396
shell bacilli.

00:29:04.438 --> 00:29:05.299
But like the little ones...

00:29:05.959 --> 00:29:07.240
At the time, people didn't let themselves be pushed around.

00:29:07.400 --> 00:29:09.302
They put us in a straitjacket,

00:29:10.303 --> 00:29:11.324
like in a madhouse.

00:29:12.345 --> 00:29:12.845
SO,

00:29:13.766 --> 00:29:14.746
a 4-year-old girl,

00:29:14.786 --> 00:29:15.347
when we do that to him,

00:29:15.487 --> 00:29:16.888
We don't really understand.

00:29:17.009 --> 00:29:32.381
We were made to write,

00:29:32.802 --> 00:29:33.942
But I didn't know how to write.

00:29:34.687 --> 00:29:36.568
So it's a monitor who's writing,

00:29:37.088 --> 00:29:45.891
So I wrote to my dad that I wished him a Merry Christmas and that for the Christmas holidays I was going to be dressed up as a little dwarf.

00:29:47.171 --> 00:29:47.671
Dear Dad,

00:29:48.111 --> 00:29:53.272
I am still in good health and I think the same is true for you.

00:29:53.972 --> 00:29:54.472
Yesterday,

00:29:54.973 --> 00:29:57.713
The cinema was very interesting.

00:29:57.894 --> 00:30:00.714
We watched a film about the mountain.

00:30:03.195 --> 00:30:03.755
We are...

00:30:04.447 --> 00:30:06.589
In the midst of preparations for the Christmas festivities,

00:30:06.789 --> 00:30:08.171
I am a little dwarf.

00:30:08.172 --> 00:30:09.892
Do you see?

00:30:13.694 --> 00:30:15.556
The little mountain dwarves,

00:30:16.396 --> 00:30:18.258
Greenish greens,

00:30:19.398 --> 00:30:21.319
They work all night,

00:30:21.819 --> 00:30:23.701
While the shepherds sleep.

00:30:29.225 --> 00:30:31.606
That's all I remember!

00:30:38.472 --> 00:30:41.014
Tuberculosis still kills 10,000 French people every year.

00:30:41.514 --> 00:30:43.336
It is therefore important to educate the public,

00:30:43.636 --> 00:30:45.057
from school onwards, but also afterwards,

00:30:45.497 --> 00:30:47.138
ways to avoid it and defend against it.

00:30:47.559 --> 00:30:50.681
Hence the importance of information and health education in this area.

00:30:51.342 --> 00:30:55.965
It is on this theme that the new anti-tuberculosis stamp campaign, presented by Georges Duhamel, has just been launched.

00:30:56.025 --> 00:30:58.547
President of the National Committee for the Defense against Tuberculosis,

00:30:58.787 --> 00:30:59.208
to Mr.

00:30:59.208 --> 00:30:59.708
Chenot,

